[list]
[*]Rival Exchanges now follow the game's own rivalries. Any rivalry where both countries have an Exchange, whichever side declared it, makes their Exchanges rivals. The separate rival choice, its Choose button, lock and End button are gone. On old saves the previous rival is simply dropped.
[*]The yearly rival contest settles once per rivalry. The winner gains 3 trust from the loser when both share a Standing. Across a Standing gap, an upset pays more and an expected win pays less. Poor relations still make the loser lose 1.5 times as much.
[*]New rival actions, with a one-year cooldown per target:
[list]
[*]Bear Raid costs 0.5% of GDP. The rival's Market Confidence falls by 6 over 6 months, twice that in a Speculative Mania. It fails more often the stronger they are, and on a failure you take the fall instead.
[*]Lure Their Capital adds two months of your pool income to your Outstanding Credit. In return, 10% of their Investment Pool income comes to you for 12 months.
[*]Press Campaign costs 25 influence for 12 months and takes 5% trust from their Exchange. It has a 30% chance of exposure, which costs relations and your own trust.
[*]Shore Up costs 0.5% of GDP and halves every rival action against you for 12 months. It can be used once every 2 years.
[/list]
[*]AI countries with an Exchange use these actions against their rivals, at most once per rivalry a year. Bold AIs act more often and cautious ones less, and an AI that was hit shores up first.
[*]When a rival acts against you, an event lets you answer in kind, shore up, or keep calm for a little trust.
[*]New Rivalries section in Finance: one row per rivalry with its direction, both scores, last year's result, the effects running on each side, the last move between you, and the four action buttons. Unavailable buttons are dimmed, and their tooltips list what is missing. Rivals are marked with the rivalry icon in the World list.
[*]Bear Raids on your market appear as Rival Actions in the Market Confidence breakdown.
[/list]
